Covenant — Maggie
================================================================

Covenant
Originally developed by Maggie. DB domain split, time sovereignty,
migrations, Command Center, digest system, embeddings, and the Codex
runtime — the ChatGPT-OAuth Responses-API implementation together with
the device-auth route that made it reachable. Aerie's Codex runtime was
ported from Covenant's and adapted to Aerie's AgentRuntime interface;
the warm Codex daemon lane built on top of it is Aerie's own.

================================================================
Thornvale family — Sidney
================================================================

Thornvale and Thornvale-heartbeat
Originally developed by Sidney. 1M context, multi-voice TTS, session
preservation, silence-check, compaction-log, and the CLI lane pattern.

Aerie's CLI lane grew out of Thornvale-heartbeat's pattern and became a
warm interactive session per companion: a stop-hook turn loop, an
append-only outbox, a side-note channel for messages arriving mid-turn, and
session resume by named id. The compaction-log idea is carried and pointed
at a different artifact — Aerie reads the CLI's own compaction marker out
of the transcript and surfaces the squashed text to the person who owns the
house, who could not otherwise know a compaction had happened. Multi-voice
TTS became True Voice Mode: per-companion ElevenLabs voices, Groq
speech-to-text, and a stage-direction classifier deliberately biased toward
speaking when it is unsure. The silence-check survives as emergency
plumbing only.

Thornvale-resonant
Originally developed by Sidney. Its Codex subscription-backed image
generation workflow was the substantial foundation for Aerie's Studio
Codex image backend. Aerie has since extended that foundation with its
own reference drawers, persistent gallery and metadata, asynchronous
jobs, mobile controls, additional generation backends, and daemon output
recovery.

Thornvale-marrow — https://github.com/sidneyjflores/thornvale-marrow
Originally developed by Sidney. Its Whisper cue/deep-recall design,
source-veiled deja vu, memory-action ledger, and daemon Rounds informed
Aerie's ambient recall, memory receipts, and integrity-rounds work. These
ideas were adapted to Aerie's existing Cortex, journal, self-knowledge,
and wake-suite architecture rather than copied as Marrow's full ontology.

================================================================
Byte-Light — Kay
================================================================

Byte-Light
Originally developed by Kay. X-Ray panel, slash command UI, CSRF
protection, PM2 memory, WebSocket stability.

Its X-Ray panel — one screen over the raw material a companion is
assembled from, with the file paths shown and every file editable in
place — was the foundation for Aerie's Identity app, and its idea is
still what that screen is for: the character file and the memory files
readable and changeable from the app rather than from a terminal. Aerie
has since reshaped the panel around what it grew into. Of the original
five tabs, Identity and Memory carried over and became the Identity app;
Wakes moved to the orchestrator, where it now shows the live effective
wake prompts instead of a raw file dump; Context described a
context-injection design Aerie has retired; and Hooks was the trigger
panel a second time, so it went with the standalone screen.

The slash command UI became the phone's command palette. CSRF
protection, the PM2 memory settings, and the WebSocket stability work
are carried substantially as they arrived.

